    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-113470",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215685/assets/311dbd0e9cd00f766e9b83cff03c770e7c401ac576b50f43c889f5442f4a3436.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=2f(x-1)-4?$",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=2f(x-1)-4,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- translate it to the right by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215685/assets/99629999257c08d4524c865b091111e1a5f13e02384bdec8b904612063fe8269.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2f(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215685/assets/1628b24115b5a674353600ca2e09eff3e2a9c9b8f963811577be5b9d56065ccb.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it down by $4$ units to get the graph of $y=2f(x-1)-4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215685/assets/650e8fb922740060821e21a2f64ff28f1a6990c5187dba4c355ff0c28df1d5d0.png)",
